13.3 Review Status

The framework is open to independent technical examination.

Reviewers may challenge:

- system architecture;
- research assumptions;
- scenario models;
- resilience calculations;
- state-transition logic;
- validation methodology;
- cascade behaviour;
- simulation fidelity;
- software implementation;
- cybersecurity;
- human factors;
- auditability;
- and research conclusions.

No reviewer, organisation, regulator, classification society, space agency or other institution should be considered to have endorsed, approved or certified the framework unless such endorsement or certification is separately and formally documented.

---

13.4 Change Management Principle

Future changes to the research framework should be traceable through:

PROPOSE → IMPLEMENT → TEST → VALIDATE → DOCUMENT → REVIEW → RELEASE

Where appropriate, each significant change should identify:

- affected component;
- reason for change;
- expected effect;
- test evidence;
- validation result;
- reviewer comments;
- limitations;
- and release identifier.

Material architectural changes should trigger appropriate regression testing.

Previous experimental results should not automatically be assumed to remain valid following a material change.

---

