13.11 Release Control

A research release should identify, where applicable:

- release identifier;
- software version;
- scenario version;
- configuration version;
- documentation version;
- major architectural changes;
- validation status;
- known limitations;
- unresolved findings;
- and review status.

A release should not imply operational certification merely because the software or documentation has been updated.

---

13.12 Future Release Principle

Any future release should clearly distinguish between:

RESEARCH CAPABILITY

and

VALIDATED OPERATIONAL CAPABILITY

No increase in software capability shall by itself constitute evidence of:

- spacecraft certification;
- flight qualification;
- operational authority;
- mission approval;
- safety assurance;
- or regulatory acceptance.

Operational claims require appropriate independent evidence and mission-specific assurance.

---

13.13 Change Impact Assessment

Before material changes are released, the project should consider their potential effect on:

- scenario behaviour;
- resilience scoring;
- state transitions;
- failsafe transitions;
- validation outputs;
- memory continuity;
- audit records;
- recovery pathways;
- human decision authority;
- cybersecurity assumptions;
- and previously reported research results.

The purpose is to identify unintended consequences before publication of a new research release.

---

13.14 Regression Requirement

Material changes should trigger appropriate regression testing.

Regression testing should examine whether previously tested behaviour remains consistent under the new configuration.

Particular attention should be given to:

- existing scenarios;
- boundary conditions;
- failure conditions;
- authority gates;
- audit trails;
- validation pathways;
- and known historical findings.

A previously successful test should not automatically be considered valid for a materially different architecture.

---

13.15 Research Artefact Preservation

Where practical, the project should preserve:

- source-code versions;
- scenario definitions;
- configuration files;
- test procedures;
- experimental results;
- audit records;
- validation records;
- release identifiers;
- review findings;
- and relevant documentation.

Preservation of research artefacts supports later reproducibility and independent examination.

---

13.16 Negative Results

The project explicitly recognises negative results as valid scientific and engineering evidence.

Examples may include:

- failure to detect an abnormal condition;
- incorrect classification;
- unnecessary escalation;
- unexpected cascade propagation;
- inconsistent recovery recommendations;
- validation failure;
- excessive operator workload;
- cybersecurity weakness;
- unexplained state transition;
- or inability to reproduce a previous result.

Such findings should not be removed merely because they conflict with the intended research proposition.

---

13.17 No Retrospective Certification

A successful research result shall not be retrospectively represented as certification.

Likewise, a successful simulation shall not be described as evidence of operational spacecraft safety unless the appropriate engineering verification, validation and assurance process has independently established that conclusion.

The distinction remains:

SIMULATION RESULT ≠ OPERATIONAL SAFETY EVIDENCE

---

13.18 Open Research Position

Sextant Protocol™ is presented as an open research proposition.

The project welcomes evidence that:

- confirms its hypotheses;
- refines its assumptions;
- identifies limitations;
- exposes deficiencies;
- or disproves elements of the proposed architecture.

Challenge is therefore considered part of the research process rather than opposition to it.

---

13.19 Review Governance

Future governance of the research framework should maintain separation between:

- development;
- experimentation;
- validation;
- review;
- publication;
- and any future operational assurance activity.

The current framework remains within the research and simulation boundary.

No operational authority is created by this document.

